The loop feels like it’s perpetually on the edge of resolution because it’s built on that classic i–VI–III–VII cycle in A minor, and the G7 never gets to land on the tonic. Instead it just bounces right back into Am7, so the ear keeps expecting a cadence that never quite arrives — it’s like the song is stuck in a perpetual motion machine.

To break that cycle, the simplest and most effective move is to swap the final G7 for something that actually resolves into the tonic. Try replacing it with E7 (or Em7) on the last two bars of every other loop. That gives 8 bars of Am7–Fmaj7–Cmaj7–G7, then 8 bars of Am7–Fmaj7–Cmaj7–E7, so the second half lands on the dominant-function chord that pulls straight home to Am.","type":"summary_text"}],"type":"reasoning","status":"completed","encrypted_content":""}
